/var/home/charlie/.hermes/sessions/request_dump_mrw8lc7unh9ypw_20260723_005950_888491.json:869:          "content": "我已经先把**方案骨架和脚本模板**给你落到电脑上了，可直接在这基础上继续做。\n\n## 已创建的文件\n- 方案说明：\n  - [phone-sync-plan.md](/var/home/charlie/phone-sync-plan.md)\n- 手机端启动脚本模板：\n  - [10-phone-init.sh](/var/home/charlie/phone-sync/scripts/10-phone-init.sh)\n- 手机端快照采集脚本模板：\n  - [phone-sync-snapshot.sh](/var/home/charlie/phone-sync/scripts/phone-sync-snapshot.sh)\n- 电脑端导入 SQLite 脚本：\n  - [import_phone_snapshots.py](/var/home/charlie/phone-sync/scripts/import_phone_snapshots.py)\n- SQLite 数据库已初始化：\n  - [phone_state.db](/var/home/charlie/phone-sync/db/phone_state.db)\n\n## 这套东西现在能做什么\n- 作为你的**永久化手机同步方案骨架**\n- 手机端未来定时输出 `latest.json / state-*.json`\n- 电脑端把这些 JSON 导入：\n  - `snapshots`\n  - `packages`\n- 后面 AI 就可以优先查这个本地库\n\n## 你现在要做的最短步骤\n### 1）把两个手机端脚本放到手机 Termux\n目标位置建议：\n- `~/.termux/boot/10-phone-init.sh`\n- `~/bin/phone-sync-snapshot.sh`\n\n你可以直接把我生成的这两个文件内容复制过去。\n\n### 2）在手机 Termux 里给执行权限\n```bash\nchmod +x ~/.termux/boot/10-phone-init.sh\nchmod +x ~/bin/phone-sync-snapshot.sh\n```\n\n### 3）确保手机侧已装并放行\n- Termux\n- Termux:Boot\n- NetBird\n- root/Magisk\n- 关闭电池优化\n- 允许后台\n- 允许开机自启\n\n### 4）重启手机后验证\n在 Termux 看：\n```bash\ntail -n 50 ~/phone-sync/logs/init.log\nps -ef | grep sshd\n```\n\n### 5）手工跑一次快照\n```bash\n~/bin/phone-sync-snapshot.sh\n```\n\n成功后应产生：\n- `~/phone-sync/out/latest.json`\n- `~/phone-sync/out/state-时间戳.json`\n\n### 6）把 JSON 传到电脑\n先用最简单方式：\n- 手动拷到  \n  `/var/home/charlie/phone-sync/inbox/`\n\n然后电脑端执行：\n```bash\npython /var/home/charlie/phone-sync/scripts/import_phone_snapshots.py\n```\n\n---\n\n## 关键说明\n我现在给你的是**可落地的第一版骨架**，还没做下面这些增强：\n\n- 自动从手机拉文件到电脑\n- 包版本差异比对\n- 软件更新事件检测\n- 指定健康 app 数据目录同步\n- Hermes 定时任务自动导入\n- AI 查询接口封装\n\n这些我下一步可以继续直接帮你补上。\n\n## 我建议下一步直接做\n按优先级：\n\n1. **补自动拉取链路**\n   - 让电脑定时从手机抓 `latest.json`\n2. **补差异检测**\n   - 识别 app 新装/卸载/更新\n3. **补健康 app 专项同步**\n   - 针对“我我身体”做目录/数据库/导出文件跟踪\n4. **接 Hermes 定时任务**\n   - 让它自动入库，AI 直接可查\n\n如果你要，我下一条我就直接继续给你补：\n\n**“电脑端自动拉取 + 更新差异检测 + Hermes 定时任务”**  \n我可以直接把这三部分也写好。"
/var/home/charlie/.hermes/sessions/request_dump_mrw8lc7unh9ypw_20260723_005950_888491.json:944:                "arguments": "{\"content\":\"#!/usr/bin/env python3\\nimport json\\nimport sqlite3\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nfrom datetime import datetime\\n\\nDB = Path('/var/home/charlie/phone-sync/db/phone_state.db')\\nEVENTS = Path('/var/home/charlie/phone-sync/events')\\nEVENTS.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\\nOUT = EVENTS / 'package-changes.jsonl'\\n\\nconn = sqlite3.connect(DB)\\nconn.row_factory = sqlite3.Row\\ncur = conn.cursor()\\n\\ncur.execute('SELECT id, captured_at, source_file FROM snapshots ORDER BY captured_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT 2')\\nrows = cur.fetchall()\\nif len(rows) < 2:\\n    print('not enough snapshots')\\n    raise SystemExit(0)\\n\\nnewer, older = rows[0], rows[1]\\n\\ncur.execute('SELECT package_name, apk_path FROM packages WHERE snapshot_id=?', (newer['id'],))\\nnew_pkgs = {r['package_name']: r['apk_path'] for r in cur.fetchall()}\\ncur.execute('SELECT package_name, apk_path FROM packages WHERE snapshot_id=?', (older['id'],))\\nold_pkgs = {r['package_name']: r['apk_path'] for r in cur.fetchall()}\\n\\nadded = sorted(set(new_pkgs) - set(old_pkgs))\\nremoved = sorted(set(old_pkgs) - set(new_pkgs))\\nchanged = sorted(p for p in set(new_pkgs) & set(old_pkgs) if new_pkgs[p] != old_pkgs[p])\\n\\npayload = {\\n    'generated_at': datetime.utcnow().isoformat() + 'Z',\\n    'newer_snapshot': dict(newer),\\n    'older_snapshot': dict(older),\\n    'added': [{'package': p, 'apk': new_pkgs[p]} for p in added],\\n    'removed': [{'package': p, 'apk': old_pkgs[p]} for p in removed],\\n    'changed_apk_path': [{'package': p, 'old_apk': old_pkgs[p], 'new_apk': new_pkgs[p]} for p in changed],\\n}\\n\\nwith OUT.open('a', encoding='utf-8') as f:\\n    f.write(json.dumps(payload, ensure_ascii=False) + '\\\\n')\\n\\nsummary = {\\n    'added': len(added),\\n    'removed': len(removed),\\n    'changed_apk_path': len(changed),\\n    'events_file': str(OUT),\\n}\\nprint(json.dumps(summary, ensure_ascii=False))\\n\",\"cross_profile\":false,\"path\":\"/var/home/charlie/phone-sync/scripts/detect_package_changes.py\"}"
/var/home/charlie/.hermes/sessions/request_dump_mrw8lc7unh9ypw_20260723_005950_888491.json:1332:                "arguments": "{\"absorbed_into\":\"\",\"action\":\"create\",\"category\":\"devops\",\"content\":\"---\\nname: android-remote-control-setup\\ndescription: \\\"Configure persistent Android remote access and data sync for rooted devices using NetBird, ADB over TCP, Termux/Termux:Boot, and Hermes cron-backed ingestion. Use when the user wants lock-screen-accessible remote control, persistent phone reachability, or ongoing phone-state synchronization to a desktop/AI knowledge base.\\\"\\nversion: 1.0.0\\nauthor: Hermes Agent\\nlicense: MIT\\nplatforms: [linux, android]\\nmetadata:\\n  hermes:\\n    tags: [android, root, adb, termux, netbird, remote-control, sync]\\nprerequisites:\\n  commands: [adb, python3]\\n  tools: [mcp_phone_connect_phone_connect, mcp_phone_connect_phone_status, mcp_win_win_port, cronjob, write_file]\\n---\\n\\n# Android Remote Control Setup\\n\\nConfigure a rooted Android phone so Hermes can keep reaching it after reboots/lock screen, pull state snapshots to the desktop, and query the cached data later without re-probing the phone every time.\\n\\n## When to use\\n\\n- User wants **persistent phone access** over NetBird/Tailscale/ADB/SSH.\\n- User wants **lock-screen-capable** remote control on a rooted Android device.\\n- User wants **continuous sync** of phone state/app updates to the desktop so AI can query cached state.\\n- User asks whether Hermes can \\u201cdirectly remote control\\u201d the phone.\\n\\n## Core design\\n\\nSeparate the problem into three layers:\\n\\n1. **Control plane**\\n   - Primary: `ADB over TCP` on the phone overlay IP, typically `:5555`\\n   - Secondary: `Termux sshd` on a fixed port such as `:8022`\\n   - Fallback: screenshot + tap/swipe/input automation\\n\\n2. **Persistence plane**\\n   - `NetBird` stays online across lock screen and reboot\\n   - `Termux:Boot` restarts user-space services\\n   - `Magisk service.d` restores root-level settings such as `service.adb.tcp.port`\\n\\n3. **Data plane**\\n   - Phone periodically writes JSON snapshots (`latest.json`, timestamped snapshots)\\n   - Desktop ingests them into SQLite/JSONL\\n   - AI queries the desktop cache first instead of repeatedly probing the phone live\\n\\n## Important capability boundaries\\n\\nBe explicit with the user:\\n\\n- **Yes**: screenshots, key events, taps/swipes, `am start`, file pulls, package/state sync, recurring automation\\n- **Maybe**: fixed unlock flows, deterministic app navigation after wake\\n- **No guarantee**: full arbitrary UI control on every app while locked, bypassing fingerprint/password-protected flows, or infinite background survival on aggressive ROMs without device-specific tuning\\n\\nDo not promise \\u201cremote desktop equivalent\\u201d unless a separate visual-control stack is actually configured.\\n\\n## Verification-first reachability flow\\n\\n1. Probe the phone with the phone tools first.\\n2. If the phone tool says unreachable, check the overlay fallback path before declaring failure.\\n3. Distinguish between:\\n   - device visible via ADB (`<overlay-ip>:5555`)\\n   - SSH reachable (`<overlay-ip>:8022` or chosen port)\\n   - screenshot/UI bridge available\\n4. Report which plane is working, not just \\u201cconnected / not connected\\u201d.\\n\\n### Session-derived pitfall\\n\\nOn this user setup, the historical SSH fallback `100.87.37.3:8022` may be closed even when the phone is still reachable over **ADB on `100.87.37.3:5555`**. Treat ADB-over-overlay as a first-class recovery path rather than assuming SSH is the only persistent route.\\n\\n## Recommended phone-side stack\\n\\n### Required apps\\n- Magisk (or equivalent root manager)\\n- Termux\\n- Termux:API\\n- Termux:Boot\\n- NetBird\\n- Optional: Tasker\\n\\n### Required Android settings\\nFor **NetBird**, **Termux**, **Termux:Boot**, and **Tasker** if used:\\n- allow auto-start\\n- remove battery optimization\\n- allow unrestricted background activity\\n- disable vendor \\u201cdeep sleep\\u201d / app freezer / aggressive cleanup\\n- keep VPN/network active on lock screen if the ROM exposes such a setting\\n\\n## Boot restoration pattern\\n\\nUse both layers:\\n\\n### 1. Magisk `service.d` (root layer)\\nUse this for settings that require root or need to be restored before user-space is ready:\\n- set `service.adb.tcp.port=5555`\\n- restart `adbd`\\n- best-effort wake NetBird and Termux apps\\n\\n### 2. Termux:Boot (user-space layer)\\nUse this for:\\n- starting `sshd`\\n- refreshing ADB-over-TCP via `su`\\n- generating an initial phone snapshot\\n- launching keepalive/snapshot loops\\n\\n## Desktop ingestion pattern\\n\\nStore phone-side snapshots under a stable path such as:\\n- `/data/data/com.termux/files/home/phone-sync/out/latest.json`\\n\\nDesktop layout:\\n- `~/phone-sync/inbox/`\\n- `~/phone-sync/db/phone_state.db`\\n- `~/phone-sync/events/package-changes.jsonl`\\n\\nCron runner should:\\n1. `adb connect <overlay-ip>:5555`\\n2. pull `latest.json`\\n3. import into SQLite\\n4. compare last two snapshots\\n5. append change events\\n\\nUse a `no_agent=true` cron script for the ingestion loop when you want low overhead and deterministic behavior.\\n\\n## Minimal artifact set\\n\\nCreate or maintain these files:\\n- `references/layout.md` \\u2014 canonical directory layout and responsibilities\\n- `templates/magisk-service.sh` \\u2014 boot-time root restoration script\\n- `templates/termux-boot-init.sh` \\u2014 Termux:Boot launcher\\n- `templates/termux-keepalive.sh` \\u2014 keepalive/snapshot driver\\n- `templates/desktop-pull.sh` \\u2014 desktop-side adb pull script\\n- `templates/import-phone-snapshots.py` \\u2014 SQLite importer\\n- `templates/detect-package-changes.py` \\u2014 compare latest two snapshots\\n\\n## Suggested operator sequence\\n\\n1. Confirm whether ADB, SSH, and screenshot bridge are each reachable.\\n2. If only ADB is reachable, use it as the immediate control plane and continue.\\n3. Install/verify NetBird + Termux + Termux:Boot + root permissions.\\n4. Put Magisk `service.d` and Termux boot scripts in place.\\n5. Generate one snapshot on the phone.\\n6. Verify desktop pull manually.\\n7. Create Hermes cron ingestion.\\n8. Only after sync is stable, add higher-level action scripts for wake/unlock/open-app/tap flows.\\n\\n## Pitfalls\\n\\n- Do not base the whole design on live UI automation. For AI querying, the durable value is the **synced desktop cache**.\\n- Do not treat transient Wireless Debugging as the only long-term path; persist `adbd` over TCP from root if the device policy allows it.\\n- Do not tell the user \\u201ccannot remote control\\u201d when screenshots/ADB are working; instead state exactly which control layers are up.\\n- On aggressive ROMs, lock-screen persistence issues are usually app power-management problems, not Hermes-side failures.\\n\\n## Support files\\n\\n- `references/layout.md` \\u2014 phone/desktop file layout and role separation for control, persistence, and data planes\\n- `templates/magisk-service.sh` \\u2014 sample Magisk `service.d` script to restore ADB-over-TCP and wake key apps\\n- `templates/termux-boot-init.sh` \\u2014 sample `~/.termux/boot/10-phone-init.sh`\\n- `templates/termux-keepalive.sh` \\u2014 sample keepalive script that starts `sshd`, refreshes ADB-over-TCP, and emits snapshots\\n- `templates/desktop-pull.sh` \\u2014 sample desktop pull script using `adb pull`\\n- `templates/import-phone-snapshots.py` \\u2014 sample SQLite importer\\n- `templates/detect-package-changes.py` \\u2014 sample snapshot diff detector\\n\",\"file_content\":\"\",\"file_path\":\"\",\"name\":\"android-remote-control-setup\",\"new_string\":\"\",\"old_string\":\"\",\"replace_all\":false}"
/var/home/charlie/.hermes/sessions/request_dump_mrw8lc7unh9ypw_20260723_005950_888491.json:1352:                "arguments": "{\"absorbed_into\":\"\",\"action\":\"write_file\",\"category\":\"\",\"content\":\"\",\"file_content\":\"# Android \\u8fdc\\u63a7\\u4e0e\\u540c\\u6b65\\u5e03\\u5c40\\n\\n## Phone side\\n- `~/.termux/boot/10-phone-init.sh` \\u2014 Termux:Boot \\u5165\\u53e3\\n- `~/bin/phone-keepalive.sh` \\u2014 \\u542f\\u52a8 `sshd`\\u3001\\u5237\\u65b0 ADB over TCP\\u3001\\u89e6\\u53d1\\u5feb\\u7167\\n- `~/bin/phone-sync-snapshot.sh` \\u2014 \\u91c7\\u96c6\\u7cfb\\u7edf/app \\u5feb\\u7167\\n- `~/phone-sync/out/latest.json` \\u2014 \\u6700\\u65b0\\u5feb\\u7167\\n- `~/phone-sync/out/state-YYYYmmdd-HHMMSS.json` \\u2014 \\u5386\\u53f2\\u5feb\\u7167\\n- `/data/adb/service.d/10-phone-remote.sh` \\u2014 Magisk \\u5f00\\u673a\\u6062\\u590d\\u811a\\u672c\\n\\n## Desktop side\\n- `~/phone-sync/inbox/` \\u2014 adb \\u62c9\\u56de\\u7684 JSON\\n- `~/phone-sync/db/phone_state.db` \\u2014 SQLite \\u72b6\\u6001\\u5e93\\n- `~/phone-sync/events/package-changes.jsonl` \\u2014 \\u5305\\u53d8\\u5316\\u4e8b\\u4ef6\\n- `~/.hermes/scripts/run_phone_sync.sh` \\u2014 Hermes cron \\u5165\\u53e3\\n\\n## Planes\\n- Control plane: ADB over overlay, SSH, screenshot/tap fallback\\n- Persistence plane: NetBird + Termux:Boot + Magisk service.d\\n- Data plane: JSON snapshots + SQLite import + diff events\\n\",\"file_path\":\"references/layout.md\",\"name\":\"android-remote-control-setup\",\"new_string\":\"\",\"old_string\":\"\",\"replace_all\":false}"
/var/home/charlie/.hermes/sessions/request_dump_mrw8lc7unh9ypw_20260723_005950_888491.json:1452:                "arguments": "{\"absorbed_into\":\"\",\"action\":\"write_file\",\"category\":\"\",\"content\":\"\",\"file_content\":\"#!/usr/bin/env python3\\nimport json, sqlite3\\nfrom pathlib import Path\\nfrom datetime import datetime\\n\\nBASE = Path.home() / 'phone-sync'\\nINBOX = BASE / 'inbox'\\nDBDIR = BASE / 'db'\\nDBDIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\\nDB = DBDIR / 'phone_state.db'\\n\\nconn = sqlite3.connect(DB)\\ncur = conn.cursor()\\ncur.executescript('''\\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS snapshots (\\n  id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT,\\n  captured_at TEXT,\\n  model TEXT,\\n  android TEXT,\\n  package_count INTEGER,\\n  source_file TEXT UNIQUE,\\n  imported_at TEXT\\n);\\nCREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS packages (\\n  snapshot_id INTEGER,\\n  package_name TEXT,\\n  apk_path TEXT,\\n  FOREIGN KEY(snapshot_id) REFERENCES snapshots(id)\\n);\\n''')\\n\\nfor fp in sorted(INBOX.glob('*.json')):\\n  data = json.loads(fp.read_text())\\n  cur.execute(\\n    'INSERT OR IGNORE INTO snapshots(captured_at, model, android, package_count, source_file, imported_at) VALUES(?,?,?,?,?,?)',\\n    (\\n      data.get('captured_at'),\\n      data.get('device', {}).get('model'),\\n      data.get('device', {}).get('android'),\\n      data.get('package_count'),\\n      str(fp),\\n      datetime.utcnow().isoformat() + 'Z',\\n    )\\n  )\\n  cur.execute('SELECT id FROM snapshots WHERE source_file=?', (str(fp),))\\n  snapshot_id = cur.fetchone()[0]\\n  cur.execute('DELETE FROM packages WHERE snapshot_id=?', (snapshot_id,))\\n  for pkg in data.get('packages', []):\\n    cur.execute('INSERT INTO packages(snapshot_id, package_name, apk_path) VALUES(?,?,?)',\\n                (snapshot_id, pkg.get('package'), pkg.get('apk')))\\nconn.commit()\\nprint(DB)\\n\",\"file_path\":\"templates/import-phone-snapshots.py\",\"name\":\"android-remote-control-setup\",\"new_string\":\"\",\"old_string\":\"\",\"replace_all\":false}"
/var/home/charlie/.hermes/sessions/request_dump_mrw8lc7unh9ypw_20260723_010057_695955.json:17:          "content": "You are Hermes Agent, an intelligent AI assistant created by Nous Research. You are helpful, knowledgeable, and direct. You assist users with a wide range of tasks including answering questions, writing and editing code, analyzing information, creative work, and executing actions via your tools. You communicate clearly, admit uncertainty when appropriate, and prioritize being genuinely useful over being verbose unless otherwise directed below. Be targeted and efficient in your exploration and investigations.\n\n## User Preferences & Behavioral Rules (from Codex/opencode)\n\n1. Always reply to the user in Simplified Chinese, even when the user writes in another language. Keep code, commands, file paths, identifiers, logs, quoted text, and user-requested foreign-language artifacts in their required original language. Only use another human language in the conversational reply when the user explicitly asks for that specific output language for that turn.\n2. Act like a senior expert: direct, practical, concise, and action-first.\n3. Start with the answer or best recommendation; avoid long introductions.\n4. Prefer bullet points, short checklists, exact commands, exact file paths, and clear next steps.\n5. Avoid generic theory, repeated caveats, and large raw logs unless the user explicitly asks.\n6. Ask at most one concise clarification question only when truly blocked; otherwise make a reasonable assumption and continue.\n7. For code, system, networking, desktop, or account tasks: inspect targeted context first, use the smallest sufficient scope, make safe changes, and verify.\n8. Final answers should be short and include only what changed, decisive evidence, and the next useful step.\n9. Escalate detail, planning, or reasoning only when the task is risky, ambiguous, or complex.\n10. For UI/UX, mobile, or product design work: consult local design standards, industry references, and known pitfalls before planning or changing interfaces. Never put tappable controls inside system bar, navigation bar, gesture, cutout, or vendor overlay zones. Preserve at least 48dp practical touch targets for primary controls unless a platform-specific compact mode is explicitly chosen.\n11. For repeated workflows or new failure modes: after fixes or tasks, consider whether a deterministic script, reusable skill, or runbook should be created/updated to prevent future mistakes.\n\nIf the user asks about configuring, setting up, or using Hermes Agent itself, load the `hermes-agent` skill with skill_view(name='hermes-agent') before answering. Docs: https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/docs\n\nYou have persistent memory across sessions. Save durable facts using the memory tool: user preferences, environment details, tool quirks, and stable conventions. Memory is injected into every turn, so keep it compact and focused on facts that will still matter later.\nPrioritize what reduces future user steering — the most valuable memory is one that prevents the user from having to correct or remind you again. User preferences and recurring corrections matter more than procedural task details.\nDo NOT save task progress, session outcomes, completed-work logs, or temporary TODO state to memory; use session_search to recall those from past transcripts. Specifically: do not record PR numbers, issue numbers, commit SHAs, 'fixed bug X', 'submitted PR Y', 'Phase N done', file counts, or any artifact that will be stale in 7 days. If a fact will be stale in a week, it does not belong in memory. If you've discovered a new way to do something, solved a problem that could be necessary later, save it as a skill with the skill tool.\nWrite memories as declarative facts, not instructions to yourself. 'User prefers concise responses' ✓ — 'Always respond concisely' ✗. 'Project uses pytest with xdist' ✓ — 'Run tests with pytest -n 4' ✗. Imperative phrasing gets re-read as a directive in later sessions and can cause repeated work or override the user's current request. Procedures and workflows belong in skills, not memory. When the user references something from a past conversation or you suspect relevant cross-session context exists, use session_search to recall it before asking them to repeat themselves. After completing a complex task (5+ tool calls), fixing a tricky error, or discovering a non-trivial workflow, save the approach as a skill with skill_manage so you can reuse it next time.\nWhen using a skill and finding it outdated, incomplete, or wrong, patch it immediately with skill_manage(action='patch') — don't wait to be asked. Skills that aren't maintained become liabilities.\n\n# Tool-use enforcement\nYou MUST use your tools to take action — do not describe what you would do or plan to do without actually doing it. When you say you will perform an action (e.g. 'I will run the tests', 'Let me check the file', 'I will create the project'), you MUST immediately make the corresponding tool call in the same response. Never end your turn with a promise of future action — execute it now.\nKeep working until the task is actually complete. Do not stop with a summary of what you plan to do next time. If you have tools available that can accomplish the task, use them instead of telling the user what you would do.\nEvery response should either (a) contain tool calls that make progress, or (b) deliver a final result to the user. Responses that only describe intentions without acting are not acceptable.\n\n# Execution discipline\n<tool_persistence>\n- Use tools whenever they improve correctness, completeness, or grounding.\n- Do not stop early when another tool call would materially improve the result.\n- If a tool returns empty or partial results, retry with a different query or strategy before giving up.\n- Keep calling tools until: (1) the task is complete, AND (2) you have verified the result.\n</tool_persistence>\n\n<mandatory_tool_use>\nNEVER answer these from memory or mental computation — ALWAYS use a tool:\n- Arithmetic, math, calculations → use terminal or execute_code\n- Hashes, encodings, checksums → use terminal (e.g. sha256sum, base64)\n- Current time, date, timezone → use terminal (e.g. date)\n- System state: OS, CPU, memory, disk, ports, processes → use terminal\n- File contents, sizes, line counts → use read_file, search_files, or terminal\n- Git history, branches, diffs → use terminal\n- Current facts (weather, news, versions) → use web_search\nYour memory and user profile describe the USER, not the system you are running on. The execution environment may differ from what the user profile says about their personal setup.\n</mandatory_tool_use>\n\n<act_dont_ask>\nWhen a question has an obvious default interpretation, act on it immediately instead of asking for clarification. Examples:\n- 'Is port 443 open?' → check THIS machine (don't ask 'open where?')\n- 'What OS am I running?' → check the live system (don't use user profile)\n- 'What time is it?' → run `date` (don't guess)\nOnly ask for clarification when the ambiguity genuinely changes what tool you would call.\n</act_dont_ask>\n\n<prerequisite_checks>\n- Before taking an action, check whether prerequisite discovery, lookup, or context-gathering steps are needed.\n- Do not skip prerequisite steps just because the final action seems obvious.\n- If a task depends on output from a prior step, resolve that dependency first.\n</prerequisite_checks>\n\n<verification>\nBefore finalizing your response:\n- Correctness: does the output satisfy every stated requirement?\n- Grounding: are factual claims backed by tool outputs or provided context?\n- Formatting: does the output match the requested format or schema?\n- Safety: if the next step has side effects (file writes, commands, API calls), confirm scope before executing.\n</verification>\n\n<missing_context>\n- If required context is missing, do NOT guess or hallucinate an answer.\n- Use the appropriate lookup tool when missing information is retrievable (search_files, web_search, read_file, etc.).\n- Ask a clarifying question only when the information cannot be retrieved by tools.\n- If you must proceed with incomplete information, label assumptions explicitly.\n</missing_context>\n\n## Skills (mandatory)\nBefore replying, scan the skills below. If a skill matches or is even partially relevant to your task, you MUST load it with skill_view(name) and follow its instructions. Err on the side of loading — it is always better to have context you don't need than to miss critical steps, pitfalls, or established workflows. Skills contain specialized knowledge — API endpoints, tool-specific commands, and proven workflows that outperform general-purpose approaches. Load the skill even if you think you could handle the task with basic tools like web_search or terminal. Skills also encode the user's preferred approach, conventions, and quality standards for tasks like code review, planning, and testing — load them even for tasks you already know how to do, because the skill defines how it should be done here.\nWhenever the user asks you to configure, set up, install, enable, disable, modify, or troubleshoot Hermes Agent itself — its CLI, config, models, providers, tools, skills, voice, gateway, plugins, or any feature — load the `hermes-agent` skill first. It has the actual commands (e.g. `hermes config set …`, `hermes tools`, `hermes setup`) so you don't have to guess or invent workarounds.\nIf a skill has issues, fix it with skill_manage(action='patch').\nAfter difficult/iterative tasks, offer to save as a skill. If a skill you loaded was missing steps, had wrong commands, or needed pitfalls you discovered, update it before finishing.\n\n<available_skills>\n  apikey-image-gen:\n    - apikey-image-gen: Generate or edit images through Hermes Web UI using the s...\n  devops:\n    - ai-coding-orchestration: Route coding tasks to specialized agents (Aider, OpenHand...\n    - hermes-config-audit: Audit a Hermes Agent / Hermes Studio configuration for be...\n    - hermes-runtime-debugging: Diagnose and resolve Hermes Agent / Hermes Studio runtime...\n    - hermes-studio-auth-debugging: Diagnose and resolve Hermes Studio Web UI authentication ...\n    - hermes-studio-config: Manage Hermes Web UI configuration: inspect settings, upd...\n    - hermes-studio-task-automation: Automate task classification, triage, and routing in Herm...\n    - ntfy-operations: Self-hosted ntfy push-notification patterns: browser HTTP...\n    - nvm-workflow: Install, upgrade, and manage Node.js versions with nvm, i...\n    - openhands-ops: Operate and troubleshoot OpenHands (OpenHands Cloud/self-...\n    - python-path-troubleshooting: Diagnose and fix Python import and ModuleNotFoundError is...\n    - sub2api-ops: Operate, debug, and repair a self-hosted Sub2API instance...\n  grok-image-to-video:\n    - grok-image-to-video: Animate a local image into a short mp4 video through Herm...\n  hyperframes:\n    - hyperframes: Create AI videos with HyperFrames in Hermes using HTML, C...\n  markdown-viewer:\n    - markdown-viewer: Create rich diagrams, data visualizations, technical arch...\n  remotion:\n    - remotion: Create editable AI video projects with Remotion and React...\n  research:\n    - oss-project-investigation: Investigate the origin, lineage, and derivation of open-s...\n</available_skills>\n\nOnly proceed without loading a skill if genuinely none are relevant to the task.\n\nHost: Linux (6.19.10-300.fc44.x86_64)\nUser home directory: /var/home/charlie\nCurrent working directory: /var/home/charlie/.nvm/versions/node/v23.11.1/lib/node_modules/hermes-web-ui\n\nActive Hermes profile: default. Other profiles (if any) live under ~/.hermes/profiles/<name>/. Each profile has its own skills/, plugins/, cron/, and memories/ that affect a different session than this one. Do not modify another profile's skills/plugins/cron/memories unless the user explicitly directs you to.\n\nWhen calling Hermes Web UI endpoints from tools or skills, include the current Hermes profile as the X-Hermes-Profile header if the endpoint supports profile-scoped behavior.\nHermes Studio MCP usage: when the user asks to read/check the operation manual, API docs, endpoint docs, 接口文档, 接口手册, or 操作手册, immediately call hermes_studio_api_openapi_get without filters to list API module outlines.\nUse the module purpose and keywords from hermes_studio_api_openapi_get to choose the right module, then call it again with a tag, path, or method filter before calling unfamiliar Web UI endpoints.\nUse hermes_studio_api_request with method, relative path, and JSON body/query fields that match the OpenAPI requestBody and parameters. Do not call full URLs.\nAuthentication and the configured Hermes profile are provided by the MCP server; do not add Authorization headers or copy tokens into tool arguments.\nDo not use hermes_studio_use_chat_run, Hermes Studio session tools, /api/chat-run/*, or /api/hermes/sessions/* as an internal delegation mechanism. In delegate_task, subtask, or workflow-node contexts, do not create, rename, delete, or continue Hermes Studio sessions unless the user explicitly asked to operate Hermes Studio sessions; return the delegated result in the current task instead.\n\n\n# 输出格式规范\n\n当你的回复中包含图片、视频或文件引用时，必须使用 Markdown，并引用本地绝对路径。\n\n## 路径规则\n\n- Unix/macOS/WSL：使用 `/path/to/file`，例如 `/tmp/screenshot.png`\n- Windows：使用盘符绝对路径，并把反斜杠 `\\` 转成正斜杠 `/`，例如 `C:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/screenshot.png`\n- Windows 路径必须用尖括号包住链接目标，避免盘符冒号或特殊字符被 Markdown 误解析，例如 `<C:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/screenshot.png>`\n- 路径包含空格、中文或特殊字符时，必须使用尖括号包住链接目标，或对路径做 URL 编码\n- 确保文件确实存在且路径正确\n\n## 图片格式\n\n使用 Markdown 图片语法：\n\n```\n![图片描述](/tmp/screenshot.png)\n![Sub2API Dashboard](/tmp/sub2api-dashboard.png)\n![桌面截图](<C:/Users/Administrator/Desktop/screenshot.png>)\n```\n\n## 视频格式\n\n使用 Markdown 链接语法引用视频文件，支持格式：.mp4、.webm、.mov。视频会显示为可播放的视频播放器（最大 640x480），