/var/home/charlie/.hermes/sessions/request_dump_mrx7k13qrulyf4_20260723_183149_144548.json:325:          "content": "现在**没有你这台安卓手机的现成软件清单**，我刚检查过了，结论很明确：\n\n- **手机当前不可连**\n  - 手机状态：`无法连接手机`\n  - `adb devices`：空\n- **已有同步链路目前没产出可用清单**\n  - `/var/home/charlie/phone-sync/db/phone_state.db` 里 `snapshots=0`\n  - `packages=0`\n- **历史同步任务一直在失败**\n  - 日志里反复是：`adb device unavailable: 100.87.37.3:5555`\n  - 说明之前确实想拉你的安装包列表，但现在没拉下来\n\n所以当前我**不能直接告诉你“你手机上有哪些 app、哪些该迁移”**，因为本地没有拿到真实清单。\n\n但我已经顺手确认了另一件事：\n\n- **你的电脑/Windows 侧在线**\n  - `wifi 192.168.123.136:22 ONLINE`\n  - `frp 127.0.0.1:2222 ONLINE`\n- **Hermes 里也没发现可直接读取的安卓设备**\n  - LAN devices: `0`\n\n### 现在最对路的做法\n先拿到**手机真实安装列表**，再帮你筛“必要迁移集”。  \n我建议按这个标准筛第一批：\n\n#### 第一批优先迁移\n- **通讯/身份**\n  - 微信、Telegram、QQ、邮箱、认证器、密码管理器\n- **浏览器与书签/登录态相关**\n  - Chrome、Edge、Firefox、Kiwi 之类\n- **工作流工具**\n  - Termux、Tasker、网盘、笔记、远程控制\n- **数据承载型应用**\n  - 相册同步、文件管理、下载器、阅读器\n- **系统能力依赖应用**\n  - VPN、代理、输入法、启动器、自动化、通知增强\n\n#### 暂时不急迁移\n- 纯内容消费类\n  - 短视频、音乐、新闻、普通视频平台\n- 可重新安装即可恢复的\n  - 大多数工具类、壁纸类、主题类\n- 明显受架构/环境限制的\n  - 依赖 root、Magisk 模块、厂商框架、Xposed/LSPosed、系统签名权限的 app\n- 强绑定真机硬件的\n  - SIM/电话/短信/NFC/生物识别/相机深度集成类\n\n### 针对“安卓模拟器”我先给你一个判断框架\n模拟器里**值得装**的通常是：\n\n- 聊天/轻办公\n- 浏览器\n- 阅读/笔记\n- 文件同步\n- 自动化测试用 app\n- 不依赖真机传感器/电话/NFC 的普通应用\n\n模拟器里**通常没必要迁移**的：\n\n- 银行/支付主力 app\n- 强风控 app\n- root/模块深度依赖 app\n- 电话、短信、eSIM、NFC、车钥匙、门禁类\n- 对相机、陀螺仪、蓝牙外设、定位精度要求高的 app\n- 游戏（除非你明确就是拿模拟器跑）\n\n### 我现在缺的唯一关键数据\n你要我继续准确筛选，下一步只需要**拿到手机安装列表**。  \n你可以二选一：\n\n#### 方案 A：你直接给我手机 app 清单\n你在手机上导出/截图/复制应用列表给我，我直接帮你分三类：\n- 必迁移\n- 可选迁移\n- 不建议迁移\n\n#### 方案 B：我来帮你把清单拉出来\n只要把手机连通，我就能继续检查并给你出第一批迁移名单。  \n目前阻塞点就是：\n\n- 手机连接失败\n- ADB 到 `100.87.37.3:5555` 不通\n\n### 结论\n- **现在没有现成的软件清单**\n- **本地同步数据库也是空的**\n- **要做精准迁移筛选，必须先拿到手机安装列表**\n- 拿到后我可以直接帮你产出：\n  - **模拟器必装清单**\n  - **可后装清单**\n  - **没必要迁移清单**\n  - **因为架构/权限/硬件原因不建议迁移的清单**\n\n如果你愿意，我下一步可以直接帮你做其中一个：\n\n1. **你把手机 app 列表发我，我直接分类**\n2. **我继续帮你排查手机连接，先把安装列表拉下来**\n\n你如果想省事，直接把**模拟器名字**（如 MuMu / LDPlayer / BlueStacks / AVD / Waydroid）和**手机应用列表截图/文本**发来，我就直接给你第一版迁移方案。"
/var/home/charlie/.hermes/sessions/request_dump_mrxdg9nk2nmh51_20260724_122603_065855.json:320:          "content": "{\"success\": true, \"name\": \"android-remote-control-setup\", \"description\": \"Configure persistent Android remote access and data sync for rooted devices using NetBird, ADB over TCP, Termux/Termux:Boot, and Hermes cron-backed ingestion. Use when the user wants lock-screen-accessible remote control, persistent phone reachability, or ongoing phone-state synchronization to a desktop/AI knowledge base.\", \"tags\": [\"android\", \"root\", \"adb\", \"termux\", \"netbird\", \"remote-control\", \"sync\"], \"related_skills\": [], \"content\": \"---\\nname: android-remote-control-setup\\ndescription: \\\"Configure persistent Android remote access and data sync for rooted devices using NetBird, ADB over TCP, Termux/Termux:Boot, and Hermes cron-backed ingestion. Use when the user wants lock-screen-accessible remote control, persistent phone reachability, or ongoing phone-state synchronization to a desktop/AI knowledge base.\\\"\\nversion: 1.0.0\\nauthor: Hermes Agent\\nlicense: MIT\\nplatforms: [linux, android]\\nmetadata:\\n  hermes:\\n    tags: [android, root, adb, termux, netbird, remote-control, sync]\\nprerequisites:\\n  commands: [adb, python3]\\n  tools: [mcp_phone_connect_phone_connect, mcp_phone_connect_phone_status, mcp_win_win_port, cronjob, write_file]\\n---\\n\\n# Android Remote Control Setup\\n\\nConfigure a rooted Android phone so Hermes can keep reaching it after reboots/lock screen, pull state snapshots to the desktop, and query the cached data later without re-probing the phone every time.\\n\\n## User communication preferences\\n- Default to **concise, action-first Chinese replies**.\\n- When the user asks \\\"怎么解决 / 怎么办 / 那我X怎么解决\\\", immediately provide **concrete device-side remediation steps ordered by success rate**.\\n- Do not re-explain Linux-side diagnostics unless the user asks or the evidence is exhausted.\\n- For Android troubleshooting, prefer **manual app/battery/VPN checklist** over asking the user to run shell commands.\\n- If the user says to continue an Android APK/input-method task, treat that as permission to modify the project, build the APK, push it to the phone, and install it without additional confirmation unless a risky choice is involved.\\n- After making APK changes, verify phone reachability first, then complete the full loop: build → install → set/enable the relevant component (for IME, `ime enable` + `ime set`) → report concise evidence only.\\n- When a project ships its own install script, inspect it if installation fails, but do not block on the script if you can build with a known-good JDK/Gradle invocation and install directly with ADB.\\n- For Android IME debugging, do not stop at successful install or `ime set`. If the user reports \\\"能选中但弹不出输入框\\\", treat it as an IME window-visibility bug and inspect live `adb shell dumpsys input_method` fields such as `mSelectedMethodId`, `mCurId`, `mInputShown`, and `mImeWindowVis` after re-selecting the IME.\\n- For custom `InputMethodService` work, first try explicit lifecycle/display fixes before invasive refactors: add/verify `onStartInput(...)` state reset, `onStartInputView(...)` theme/layout rebuild, `setCandidatesViewShown(true)` when appropriate, and `onEvaluateInputViewShown() = true`, then rebuild/install and re-check `dumpsys input_method`.\\n- When the user reports an Android IME issue, continue driving the full debug loop until the keyboard actually shows or you have concrete live evidence of what still blocks it; do not hand off testing as the stopping point if more deterministic inspection remains possible.\\n- If the user says to continue an Android APK/input-method task, treat that as permission to modify the project, build the APK, push it to the phone, and install it without additional confirmation unless a risky choice is involved.\\n- After making APK changes, verify phone reachability first, then complete the full loop: build → install → set/enable the relevant component (for IME, `ime enable` + `ime set`) → report concise evidence only.\\n- When a project ships its own install script, inspect it if installation fails, but do not block on the script if you can build with a known-good JDK/Gradle invocation and install directly with ADB.\\n- For Android IME debugging, do not stop at successful install or `ime set`. If the user reports \\\"能选中但弹不出输入框\\\", treat it as an IME window-visibility bug and inspect live `adb shell dumpsys input_method` fields such as `mSelectedMethodId`, `mCurId`, `mInputShown`, and `mImeWindowVis` after re-selecting the IME.\\n- For custom `InputMethodService` work, first try explicit lifecycle/display fixes before invasive refactors: add/verify `onStartInput(...)` state reset, `onStartInputView(...)` theme/layout rebuild, `setCandidatesViewShown(true)` when appropriate, and `onEvaluateInputViewShown() = true`, then rebuild/install and re-check `dumpsys input_method`.\\n- When the user reports an Android IME issue, continue driving the full debug loop until the keyboard actually shows or you have concrete live evidence of what still blocks it; do not hand off testing as the stopping point if more deterministic inspection remains possible.\\n\\n## When to use\\n\\n- User wants **persistent phone access** over NetBird/Tailscale/ADB/SSH.\\n- User wants **lock-screen-capable** remote control on a rooted Android device.\\n- User wants **continuous sync** of phone state/app updates to the desktop so AI can query cached state.\\n- User asks whether Hermes can “directly remote control” the phone.\\n- User wants to **debug or configure an MCP server running on the phone** (for example `android-remote-control-mcp` on `127.0.0.1:8080`), including auth/session/cookie/event-channel setup.\\n\\n## Core design\\n\\nSeparate the problem into three layers:\\n\\n1. **Control plane**\\n   - Primary: `ADB over TCP` on the phone overlay IP, typically `:5555`\\n   - Secondary: `Termux sshd` on a fixed port such as `:8022`\\n   - Fallback: screenshot + tap/swipe/input automation\\n\\n2. **Persistence plane**\\n   - `NetBird` stays online across lock screen and reboot\\n   - `Termux:Boot` restarts user-space services\\n   - `Magisk service.d` restores root-level settings such as `service.adb.tcp.port`\\n\\n3. **Data plane**\\n   - Phone periodically writes JSON snapshots (`latest.json`, timestamped snapshots)\\n   - Desktop ingests them into SQLite/JSONL\\n   - AI queries the desktop cache first instead of repeatedly probing the phone live\\n\\n## Important capability boundaries\\n\\nBe explicit with the user:\\n\\n- **Yes**: screenshots, key events, taps/swipes, `am start`, file pulls, package/state sync, recurring automation\\n- **Maybe**: fixed unlock flows, deterministic app navigation after wake\\n- **No guarantee**: full arbitrary UI control on every app while locked, bypassing fingerprint/password-protected flows, or infinite background survival on aggressive ROMs without device-specific tuning\\n\\nDo not promise “remote desktop equivalent” unless a separate visual-control stack is actually configured.\\n\\n## Verification-first reachability flow\\n\\n1. Probe the phone with the phone tools first.\\n2. If the phone tool says unreachable, check the overlay fallback path before declaring failure.\\n3. Distinguish between:\\n   - device visible via ADB (`<overlay-ip>:5555`)\\n   - SSH reachable (`<overlay-ip>:8022` or chosen port)\\n   - screenshot/UI bridge available\\n4. Report which plane is working, not just “connected / not connected”.\\n5. For APK installs or other long-running ADB actions, **check whether the package already landed before retrying a full reinstall**. ADB may go offline during `install` or `install-commit`, especially over FRP/TCP, while the package manager continues in the background.\\n6. If the device drops offline mid-install:\\n   - reconnect first\\n   - verify package presence with `pm path <package>` or package listing in the correct user/profile\\n   - only restart the install session if package verification fails\\n7. Prefer **staging APKs with `pm install-create` / `install-write` / `install-commit`** for large APKs on unstable remote links, because it separates transfer from commit and gives clearer recovery points than a single `adb install -r`.\\n8. For IME APKs, finish with explicit activation verification:\\n   - `ime enable <ime-component>`\\n   - `ime set <ime-component>`\\n   - `settings get secure default_input_method`\\n   - `dumpsys package <package> | grep -E 'versionCode|versionName' | head -n 2`\\n\\n### Session-derived pitfall\\n\\nOn this user setup, the historical SSH fallback `100.87.37.3:8022` may be closed even when the phone is still reachable over **ADB on `100.87.37.3:5555`**. Treat ADB-over-overlay as a first-class recovery path rather than assuming SSH is the only persistent route.\\n\\n### Session-derived pitfall: FRP/ADB installs\\n\\nDuring remote APK installation over `127.0.0.1:15555`, `adb install -r` timed out and a subsequent staged `pm install-commit` lost the device because ADB went offline. The durable lesson is not the exact error but the recovery order: **reconnect, check whether the app is already installed for the intended user/profile, then resume or restart commit only if absent**. Do not tell the user installation failed until package presence has been checked after reconnect.\\n\\n## Recommended phone-side stack\\n\\n### Required apps\\n- Magisk (or equivalent root manager)\\n- Termux\\n- Termux:API\\n- Termux:Boot\\n- NetBird\\n- Optional: Tasker\\n\\n### Required Android settings\\nFor **NetBird**, **Termux**, **Termux:Boot**, and **Tasker** if used:\\n- allow auto-start\\n- remove battery optimization\\n- allow unrestricted background activity\\n- disable vendor “deep sleep” / app freezer / aggressive cleanup\\n- keep VPN/network active on lock screen if the ROM exposes such a setting\\n\\n## Boot restoration pattern\\n\\nUse both layers:\\n\\n### 1. Magisk `service.d` (root layer)\\nUse this for settings that require root or need to be restored before user-space is ready:\\n- set `service.adb.tcp.port=5555`\\n- restart `adbd`\\n- best-effort wake NetBird and Termux apps\\n\\n### 2. Termux:Boot (user-space layer)\\nUse this for:\\n- starting `sshd`\\n- refreshing ADB-over-TCP via `su`\\n- generating an initial phone snapshot\\n- launching keepalive/snapshot loops\\n\\n## Desktop ingestion pattern\\n\\nStore phone-side snapshots under a stable path such as:\\n- `/data/data/com.termux/files/home/phone-sync/out/latest.json`\\n\\nDesktop layout:\\n- `~/phone-sync/inbox/`\\n- `~/phone-sync/db/phone_state.db`\\n- `~/phone-sync/events/package-changes.jsonl`\\n\\nCron runner should:\\n1. `adb connect <overlay-ip>:5555`\\n2. pull `latest.json`\\n3. import into SQLite\\n4. compare last two snapshots\\n5. append change events\\n\\nUse a `no_agent=true` cron script for the ingestion loop when you want low overhead and deterministic behavior.\\n\\n## Minimal artifact set\\n\\nCreate or maintain these files:\\n- `references/layout.md` — canonical directory layout and responsibilities\\n- `templates/magisk-service.sh` — boot-time root restoration script\\n- `templates/termux-boot-init.sh` — Termux:Boot launcher\\n- `templates/termux-keepalive.sh` — keepalive/snapshot driver\\n- `templates/desktop-pull.sh` — desktop-side adb pull script\\n- `templates/import-phone-snapshots.py` — SQLite importer\\n- `templates/detect-package-changes.py` — compare latest two snapshots\\n\\n## State-machine architecture for rooted phones\\n\\nFor rooted phones with **Tasker + MacroDroid** already available, prefer a three-tier design:\\n\\n1. **Root / daemon tier** — lowest-level watchdog and repair primitives\\n   - keep `adbd` over TCP restorable\\n   - keep NetBird alive\\n   - keep Termux services alive\\n   - relax battery/background restrictions for the automation stack\\n   - expose a single repair entrypoint such as `/data/local/tmp/phone-watchdog.sh`\\n2. **Tasker tier** — authoritative state machine and orchestration\\n   - owns the canonical phone mode variable\\n   - reacts to boot, network change, Wi-Fi connect/disconnect, mobile-data changes, periodic heartbeat\\n   - chooses which repair routine to call\\n3. **MacroDroid tier** — fast event reactions and OEM-quirk handling\\n   - app-killed relaunches\\n   - screen/unlock-triggered keepalive nudges\\n   - simple UI-driven recovery actions when vendor ROM behavior is easier to automate there\\n\\n### Canonical phone states\\n\\nNormalize phone conditions into a small durable set:\\n\\n- `home_wifi_lan`\\n- `portable_wifi`\\n- `cellular_only`\\n- `netbird_overlay`\\n- `frp_adb_only`\\n- `fully_unreachable`\\n\\nDo not model every network permutation separately. The state machine should reflect **which control and return paths are usable**, not merely what radio is enabled.\\n\\n### Control-path priority\\n\\nUse a stable priority order:\\n\\n1. `FRP ADB` (e.g. `127.0.0.1:15555`) when locally exposed and healthy\\n2. `NetBird ADB` (e.g. `100.87.x.x:5555`) as the main cross-network control path\\n3. `home Wi-Fi ADB` (`192.168.x.x:5555`) only when the phone is actually on the home LAN\\n4. `Termux SSH / local agent`\\n5. `Tasker / MacroDroid local repair`\\n6. `root watchdog`\\n\\n### Important monitoring rule\\n\\nWhen the phone is reachable over **NetBird ADB** or **FRP ADB**, treat it as **controllable** even if phone→desktop LAN services such as local ntfy or clipboard ports are unreachable. In portable Wi-Fi / cellular / overlay scenarios, mark those LAN return-path checks as **notice / informational** rather than `degraded`.\\n\\n### Scenario classification rule\\n\\nWhen identifying the current phone mode, use actual interface/route evidence rather than SSID names alone:\\n\\n- `wlan0` up + home gateway → `home_wifi_lan`\\n- `wlan0` up + non-home gateway → `portable_wifi`\\n- mobile-data iface only (`rmnet*` / `ccmni*`) → `cellular_only`\\n- NetBird/TUN interface present and usable → `netbird_overlay`\\n- only FRP ADB healthy → `frp_adb_only`\\n- none of the above control paths healthy → `fully_unreachable`\\n\\nA phone connected to a portable hotspot can still report `wlan0 UP`; do not confuse “Wi-Fi connected” with “same LAN as the desktop”.\\n\\n### Recommended Tasker globals\\n\\n- `%PHONE_MODE`\\n- `%ADB_MAIN`\\n- `%NB_OK`\\n- `%ADB_TCP_OK`\\n- `%TERMUX_OK`\\n- `%WIFI_SSID`\\n- `%WIFI_GATEWAY`\\n- `%WLAN_IP`\\n\\n### Recommended Tasker triggers\\n\\n- boot completed\\n- network changed\\n- Wi-Fi connected / disconnected\\n- mobile data state changed\\n- periodic heartbeat (for example every 5 minutes)\\n- screen-unlocked or post-wake keepalive checks if the ROM is aggressive\\n\\n### Recommended root watchdog responsibilities\\n\\nA single root-entrypoint script should:\\n\\n- ensure `service.adb.tcp.port`\\n- restart `adbd` when unreachable\\n- check NetBird process / tunnel health\\n- check Termux service health\\n- relaunch Tasker / MacroDroid companion pieces if needed\\n- emit a compact JSON health snapshot for the desktop monitor to consume\\n\\n### Session-derived pitfall\\n\\nDo not classify the whole system as `degraded` merely because phone→desktop LAN services fail while the phone is on portable Wi-Fi or cellular. In this user environment, that created false-negative alerts even though **FRP ADB** and/or **NetBird ADB** still provided real control.\\n\\n\\n## MCP server auth debugging on the phone\\n\\nUse this when an Android MCP server returns `401 unauthorized`, `Method Not Allowed: SSE not supported, use POST`, or `Bad Request: Server not initialized` after you already sent `initialize`.\\n\\n### Canonical pattern for `android-remote-control-mcp`\\n\\nThis server (v1.9.0 observed) uses:\\n- **Bearer token** in `Authorization` header\\n- **Session cookie/header** `mcp-session-id` returned from `initialize` as `mcp-session-id` in response headers\\n- **Accept header** must include both `application/json` and `text/event-stream`\\n\\nSequence:\\n1. `POST /mcp` with `initialize`\\n2. Capture `mcp-session-id` from response headers\\n3. All subsequent calls send both `Authorization: Bearer *** and `mcp-session-id: <id>`\\n\\n### Phone-shell quoting survival guide\\n\\n`mcp_phone_connect_phone_shell` runs through `/system/bin/sh`. Complex curl commands with multiple `-H` headers are easily mangled. Prefer this order:\\n\\n1. **Write request body to a file first**\\n   ```sh\\n   cat <<'EOF' > /data/local/tmp/req_body.json\\n   {\\\"jsonrpc\\\":\\\"2.0\\\",\\\"id\\\":1,\\\"method\\\":\\\"initialize\\\",...}\\n   EOF\\n   ```\\n2. **Use single-line curl with `-d @file.json`**\\n   ```sh\\n   curl -sk -X POST \\\\\\n     -H \\\"Content-Type: application/json\\\" \\\\\\n     -H \\\"Accept: application/json, text/event-stream\\\" \\\\\\n     -H \\\"Authorization: Bearer *** \\\\\\n     https://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp \\\\\\n     -d @/data/local/tmp/req_body.json \\\\\\n     -w \\\"HTTP_CODE:%{http_code}\\\\n\\\"\\n   ```\\n3. **If the tool still mangles quotes**, write the full command to a script file with `printf '%s\\\\n'` and execute the script. Do not rely on heredocs with `<<EOF` or `<<'EOF'` — the device shell has repeatedly stripped or broken them.\\n\\n### Efficient diagnosis order\\n\\n1. `curl -sk -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\\\\n' https://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp` — confirms listener is up\\n2. `curl -sk https://127.0.0.1:8080/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp` — confirms auth method and scopes\\n3. `curl -sk https://127.0.0.1:8080/health` — if exposed, confirms app is healthy\\n4. `POST initialize` with proper Accept + Bearer — confirms auth path and returns session info\\n5. `POST tools/list` with same token + `mcp-session-id` — confirms session binding works\\n\\n### Common failure modes\\n\\n| Symptom | Likely cause | Fix |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `401 unauthorized` immediately | Missing/invalid Bearer, or Accept header not accepted | Add `Accept: application/json, text/event-stream` and exact Bearer |\\n| `405 Method Not Allowed: SSE not supported, use POST` | Hit `/mcp` with GET | Use `POST` |\\n| `Bad Request: Server not initialized` | Sent `tools/list` without `initialize`, or session expired | Re-run `initialize`, capture new `mcp-session-id` |\\n| `Server not initialized` after `initialize` worked | Session header missing on follow-up | Include `mcp-session-id` from initialize response |\\n\\n### Verification\\n\\nAfter successful `initialize`, expect:\\n```json\\n{\\\"id\\\":1,\\\"result\\\":{\\\"protocolVersion\\\":\\\"2024-11-05\\\",\\\"capabilities\\\":{\\\"tools\\\":{\\\"listChanged\\\":false}},\\\"serverInfo\\\":{\\\"name\\\":\\\"android-remote-control-mcp\\\",\\\"version\\\":\\\"1.9.0\\\"}}}\\n```\\n\\nAfter successful `tools/list`, expect a tools array or `{\\\"error\\\":...}` with a non-401/400 code.\\n\\n## Waydroid as the canonical master node\\n\\nWhen the user has a Linux host with reliable remote access to **Waydroid**, prefer a **single-master architecture**:\\n\\n- **Waydroid** = canonical Android master (`android-master`)\\n- **one physical phone** = viewer/controller (`android-viewer-controller`)\\n- **desktop/server** = orchestrator and repair plane\\n\\nThis is preferable to distributing duties across several backup phones when:\\n\\n- the Linux host is more reachable than the phone fleet\\n- the user wants to avoid cross-system drift\\n- the Android app can run inside Waydroid and act as the authoritative data source\\n- the phone is mainly a human interface that receives and mirrors master-state\\n\\n### Responsibilities in the final architecture\\n\\n#### 1. Waydroid (`android-master`)\\nOwns:\\n- the main APK instance\\n- authoritative task/data state\\n- versioned `master-state.json`\\n- command execution\\n- notification production\\n- cloud fallback when the phone is offline\\n\\n#### 2. Physical phone (`android-viewer-controller`)\\nOwns:\\n- UI display of master data\\n- user interaction and command submission\\n- ntfy reception\\n- Tasker / MacroDroid local reactions\\n- local cache only; **not authoritative writes**\\n\\n#### 3. Desktop/server (`orchestrator`)\\nOwns:\\n- starting/stopping/checking Waydroid\\n- bridging phone commands toward Waydroid\\n- pulling master state from Waydroid\\n- classifying alerts and recoveries\\n- long-term logs, JSONL/SQLite history, and remote maintenance\\n\\n### Architectural rule: single master, command replay, state fan-out\\n\\nUse this flow:\\n\\n- **state flow**: `Waydroid -> desktop/orchestrator -> phone`\\n- **command flow**: `phone -> desktop/orchestrator -> Waydroid`\\n- **maintenance flow**: `desktop/orchestrator -> Waydroid`\\n\\nDo **not** let the phone and Waydroid both write business state independently. The phone may cache or stage actions, but the committed state must come from the master node.\\n\\n### Why this beats multi-phone role splitting\\n\\nPrefer a single physical phone plus Waydroid master when:\\n\\n- multiple backup phones would mostly duplicate viewer duties\\n- the host machine can always repair/restart Waydroid\\n- the user wants one mobile endpoint rather than a fleet of differentiated phones\\n- the cloud/desktop-controlled Android environment should remain usable even if the physical phone changes networks or disappears temporarily\\n\\n### Practical pattern from this session\\n\\nIf the user already has host-side components such as:\\n- a Tasker/phone bridge service on the desktop\\n- a Waydroid notification relay\\n- direct Linux control over the Waydroid host\\n\\nthen treat those as the basis of the final architecture instead of building peer-to-peer phone-only recovery paths first.\\n\\n### Important pitfall\\n\\nDo not keep designing extra `rescue-phone`, `observer-phone`, and `utility-phone` roles once the user explicitly decides to collapse everything into **one phone + one Waydroid master**. After that decision, all planning should optimize:\\n\\n- master stability in Waydroid\\n- a single mobile control surface\\n- desktop-mediated command and state routing\\n\\n### Recommended canonical identifiers\\n\\n- `waydroid-main` — the only master Android node\\n- `phone-main` — the only physical viewer/controller phone\\n- `fedora-orchestrator` — the desktop/server control plane\\n\\n### Waydroid session/container split\\n\\nIn this user's setup, a running `waydroid container start` process did **not** imply a usable master Android node. The durable pattern is to model Waydroid in four layers:\\n\\n1. `container_ok` — backend/container process exists\\n2. `session_ok` — Android user session is actually started (`waydroid status` must not remain `Session: STOPPED`)\\n3. `adb_ok` — the configured local serial (for example `127.0.0.1:15555`) answers quickly\\n4. `app_ok` — the authoritative APK is running\\n\\nDo not call Waydroid healthy until all required layers for the chosen role are ready.\\n\\n### Waydroid startup pitfall\\n\\nIf `sudo waydroid session start` fails with a missing `WAYLAND_DISPLAY` / missing Wayland socket, that is evidence that **session startup belongs to the desktop user session**, not a root shell. Root/system context may still manage lower-level container/backend concerns, but session-dependent relays and app watchdogs must wait for a real user session.\\n\\n### Relay-gating rule\\n\\nHost-side relays such as notification pollers must not busy-fail against `adb -s 127.0.0.1:15555` when Waydroid session startup is incomplete. Gate them on `session_ok && adb_ok`, or classify the condition explicitly as `session_down` instead of surfacing misleading ADB timeouts.\\n\\n### Single-phone architecture rule\\n\\nOnce the user collapses the design to **one phone + one Waydroid master**, stop planning extra rescue/observer phones. Optimize for:\\n\\n- `waydroid-main` stability\\n- `phone-main` as the only viewer/controller\\n- desktop-mediated command routing and state fan-out\\n\\nWhen NetBird is up but peers stay `Idle` / no WireGuard handshake occurs:\\n\\n1. `netbird status --json` — confirm the peer is known to management, note its NetBird IP and status\\n2. `wg show <iface>` — check peer `endpoint`. If it is `127.*` or missing, ICE/guard negotiation failed\\n3. Compare DNS for multiple NetBird domains:\\n   - `api.netbird.io` (management)\\n   - `signal.netbird.io` (signaling / ICE candidates)\\n   - `relay.netbird.io` (relay fallback)\\n4. TLS-test the resolved IPs directly with `openssl s_client -connect <ip>:443 -servername <domain>`\\n   - One domain timing out while another works = selective interception / route-level block\\n5. `curl --proxy http://127.0.0.1:<local-proxy-port> https://<domain>/healthz`\\n   - Works when `curl --noproxy '*'` times out = local proxy is viable workaround\\n6. Do not assume the phone is at fault when:\\n   - peer is visible in management\\n   - phone-side logs show `activity detected` but no session\\n   - the failure is symmetric from both ends\\n\\n## Pitfalls\\n\\n- Do not base the whole design on live UI automation. For AI querying, the durable value is the **synced desktop cache**.\\n- Do not treat transient Wireless Debugging as the only long-term path; persist `adbd` over TCP from root if the device policy allows it.\\n- Do not tell the user “cannot remote control” when screenshots/ADB are working; instead state exactly which control layers are up.\\n- On aggressive ROMs, lock-screen persistence issues are usually app power-management problems, not Hermes-side failures.\\n- When debugging iOS NetBird from Linux, do not ask the user to operate the iPhone. Diagnose from the desktop using `netbird status --json`, `wg show`, DNS/TLS probes, and management logs; only fall back to asking the user to toggle airplane mode / switch networks when the desktop evidence is exhausted.\\n- For NetBird issues, do not start with “check setup key” unless `netbird status` shows an auth error. `LoginFailed`/`LoginExpired` are key-related; `DeadlineExceeded` / `Idle` peers / ICE timeouts are almost always network/routing.\\n- When the project is an Android **input method / IME**, after install also validate the user-facing activation path: open the app settings if needed, but prefer command verification first (`ime enable`, `ime set`, `settings get secure default_input_method`, package version check).\\n- For Android feature work that touches an IME, prefer a complete delivery loop in one session: inspect the project structure, patch the code, build the APK, install it, switch the active IME, and only then report the result.\\n- If the project contains a local desktop-side companion service used by the APK (for example a sync/AI bridge), inspect and patch that service in the same session when the user asks for \\\"cloud\\\" or sync-backed features. Verify with a local health check and one real endpoint request before rebuilding the APK.\\n- For snippet/TextExpander-like IME features, treat settings persistence + trigger-mode control + safe-context gating + cloud sync as one class of work rather than separate one-off edits. Implement or verify all of these together when the user asks for a production-usable expansion feature.\\n- If the user reports \\\"installed but keyboard does not pop up\\\" after an IME APK push, do not stop at `adb install` / `ime set`. Reproduce the user-facing path and verify all three layers separately: (1) package installed, (2) IME selected by the system (`settings get secure default_input_method`, `dumpsys input_method`), and (3) input window actually shown (`mInputShown`, `mImeWindowVis`) while a real text field has focus. Absence of a crash log does not clear the issue; treat \\\"selected but not shown\\\" as a distinct class of IME failure.\\n- When the user asks to \\\"一起做掉\\\" a bug fix plus Android feature work, prioritize restoring basic usability first (for IMEs: input view must open), then continue the requested feature bundle in the same delivery loop.\\n- When reporting Android APK progress for this user, distinguish clearly between \\\"installed to device\\\" and \\\"already working in hand\\\". If only ADB install/enable/set has been verified, say that explicitly and avoid implying the user has already received a working on-device experience.\\n\\n## Support files\\n\\n- `references/layout.md` — phone/desktop file layout and role separation for control, persistence, and data planes\\n- `references/android-remote-control-mcp-auth.md` — `android-remote-control-mcp` auth flow, session-id requirement, and phone-shell quoting workarounds\\n- `references/netbird-troubleshooting.md` — Linux-side NetBird connectivity diagnostics, DNS/TLS probes, and mobile peer troubleshooting without device-side shell access\\n- `references/ios-netbird-signaling.md` — iOS-specific signaling failure pattern and remediation order\\n- `references/android-netbird-signaling.md` — Android-specific signaling failure pattern, historical-session detection, and remediation order\\n- `references/termux-command-queue-and-root-validation.md` — SSH-first command-queue pattern, explicit `su -c id` root validation, and deployment-state pitfalls for rooted Termux remote-control setups\\n- `templates/magisk-service.sh` — sample Magisk `service.d` script to restore ADB-over-TCP and wake key apps\\n- `templates/termux-boot-init.sh` — sample `~/.termux/boot/10-phone-init.sh`\\n- `templates/termux-keepalive.sh` — sample keepalive script that starts `sshd`, refreshes ADB-over-TCP, and emits snapshots\\n- `templates/desktop-pull.sh` — sample desktop pull script using `adb pull`\\n- `templates/import-phone-snapshots.py` — sample SQLite importer\\n- `templates/detect-package-changes.py` — sample snapshot diff detector\\n\", \"path\": \"devops/android-remote-control-setup/SKILL.md\", \"skill_dir\": \"/var/home/charlie/.hermes/skills/devops/android-remote-control-setup\", \"linked_files\": {\"references\": [\"references/layout.md\", \"references/waydroid-master-single-phone.md\", \"references/termux-command-queue-and-root-validation.md\", \"references/ios-netbird-signaling.md\", \"references/android-netbird-signaling.md\", \"references/netbird-troubleshooting.md\", \"references/android-remote-control-mcp-auth.md\"], \"templates\": [\"templates/import-phone-snapshots.py\", \"templates/magisk-service.sh\", \"templates/termux-boot-init.sh\", \"templates/termux-keepalive.sh\", \"templates/desktop-pull.sh\"], \"scripts\": [\"scripts/check-netbird-peer-state.sh\"]}, \"usage_hint\": \"To view linked files, call skill_view(name, file_path) where file_path is e.g. 'references/api.md' or 'assets/config.yaml'\", \"required_environment_variables\": [], \"required_commands\": [], \"missing_required_environment_variables\": [], \"missing_credential_files\": [], \"missing_required_commands\": [], \"setup_needed\": false, \"setup_skipped\": false, \"readiness_status\": \"available\", \"metadata\": {\"hermes\": {\"tags\": [\"android\", \"root\", \"adb\", \"termux\", \"netbird\", \"remote-control\", \"sync\"]}}}",
